Zmedia version1
==============

Zmedia is Zero's own bounded disk-image container, file suffix .zmedia.
It is not ISO9660 and is not an ISO renamed. Its4096-byte header precedes a
complete512-sector disk payload. The flash writer validates the full container,
then writes only the payload to the target medium. PC firmware sees that disk
layout, not the Zmedia header. Firmware compatibility is supplied by the original
Zero BIOS/UEFI loaders and compatible disk partition/boot structures.

All integral fields are unsigned little-endian64-bit values.
Offset0: eight literal ASCII bytes ZMEDIA01.
Offset8: format version, exactly1.
Offset16: header length, exactly4096.
Offset24: logical payload sector size, exactly512.
Offset32: payload bytes,512 through268435456 inclusive; multiple of512.
Offset40: flags, exactly0 in version1.
Offset48:32 bytes of SHA-256 digest of the complete payload.
Offset80 through4095: reserved; every byte must be zero.
Offset4096: payload begins. Exact file size must be4096+payload bytes.

No trailing extension, compression, encryption or alternate byte order is
accepted in version1. Unknown versions, nonzero reserved bytes, odd lengths,
truncation and checksum mismatch are errors. Digest verifies content integrity;
it is not a signature, publisher identity or protection against intentional
replacement of both image and digest.

Native codec
------------
zero-media make RAW NEW.zmedia
zero-media verify FILE.zmedia
zero-media info FILE.zmedia
zero-media extract FILE.zmedia NEW_RAW

The same Zero source builds Linux x86_64 ELF and Windows x86_64 PE. File I/O uses
our compiler's direct OS interoperability primitives. No external executable or
third-party codec is invoked. The input must be a regular file; final symlinks
and reparse points are rejected. Destination creation is exclusive. Existing
outputs are preserved. After write and flush, all output bytes and EOF are read
back and compared. An output that fails verification is retained for inspection;
the codec will not unlink a path that another process may have replaced.
The source should remain unchanged while captured into memory. File length and
EOF are checked before and after reading; no filesystem snapshot is claimed.

Memory is bounded by payload plus98304 bytes for make; container size plus98304
for verify/info/extract. The current release deliberately limits images to256MiB.
The codec never opens a block device and never writes a physical disk. Physical
recording is the separate zero-flash program with target identity checks.

Shared worker ABI
-----------------
Include format-declarations.zero.part (12 vars). Set @zm to a zeroed90112-byte
arena, then include format-worker.zero.part once to create the coroutine.
Descriptor words: input pointer at0; input length8; operation16; status24;
verified payload pointer32; verified payload length40; computed digest48..79.
Operation0 validates a complete container. Operation1 hashes raw memory with
length0..268435456. Raw empty input is allowed. Other operations fail.
Initialize inputs, then !switch[@zm+128|@zm+224].
Status0 is success;65 is invalid input. Failed validation leaves output pointer
and length zero. A computed digest may be present on a checksum failure.
Caller and worker coroutine contexts occupy128 and224; SHA scratch512..1151;
worker stack4096..86015 (81920bytes). The full arena is90112bytes.
The caller owns allocated input memory and must keep it stable through the call.
This worker performs no host file I/O and uses the same logic in firmware.

Source reconstruction
---------------------
Build media/compose-codec.zero with the original Zero compiler.
compose-codec PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_SOURCE.zero reconstructs media/codec.zero.
Adding --check reconstructs media/check.zero. Both outputs were compared exactly.
Compile codec.zero --host or --windows. The supported CLI compiler uses
zero SOURCE OUTPUT --host (or --windows). The raw bootstrap compiler accepts
zero --host < SOURCE > OUTPUT; those shell redirections are host operations.

Verification
------------
media/check.zero runs8712 assertions against the exact shared worker:
4 published SHA vectors (empty,abc,56-byte standard vector,one million a bytes);
1 valid envelope and returned pointer/length;
4096 single-byte changes covering every header byte;
4608 truncations of a4608-byte valid envelope;
1 appended byte;1 payload bit flip;1 invalid operation.
All8712 passed natively on Linux and through Windows/Wine.
Independent CLI and SHA edge-vector results are recorded separately.
